 ## check.sh
 
-### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure
+### §check-fails-closed — an internal failure aborts the check with a non-zero exit
+- Since: 2026-10-22
+- Supersedes: §check-fails-open
+- Decision: `set -euo pipefail`, no trailing `exit 0`. Any failing command aborts the script with a non-zero exit.
+  Large staged files are still only a warning (exit 0).
+- Forbidden: removing `-e`, or masking failures with a final `exit 0` / `|| true`.
+- Reason: maintainer call. Under fail-open, breakage inside the check went unnoticed.
+- Note: `[ … ] && cmd` as the last command of the loop body makes the loop return 1 whenever the test is false,
+  which aborts under `-e`. Use `if … then … fi` there. Staged-then-deleted files (the 2026-09-10 incident) are
+  skipped by the `[ -f "$f" ] || continue` guard.
+
+### §check-fails-open — the check never blocks a commit because of its own failure (superseded)
+- Superseded: 2026-10-22 by §check-fails-closed
 - Since: 2026-09-11
 - Decision: `set -uo pipefail` without `-e`. Every internal error still ends in `exit 0`; the script only warns.
 - Forbidden: `set -e` or `set -o errexit`, and any exit status other than 0.
